  "strategy": "I need to design a step function sequence minimizer with a strict time budget, considering the non-convex nature of the search space. The core objective is to find a sequence of non-negative floats that minimizes the evaluation function: 2 * n * max(full convolution) / (sum of sequence)^2.\n\nMy approach combines three distinct heuristic phases to balance exploration and exploitation while respecting time constraints:\n1. First, I focus on sequences with a fixed 512 elements, using methods from the previous program as a baseline\n2. Then, I explore 1000-element sequences using gradient-based fine-tuning with analytic speed-ups\n3. Finally, I attempt to construct sequences of variable length, combining the best properties from earlier phases\n\nThe algorithm design leverages:\n- Logarithmic barriers to handle non-negativity constraints\n- Binary search for scaling properties\n- Phase separation to avoid premature halting of optimization\n- Progressive tightening to capture stable minima while allowing escape from local optima\n- Transient-specific hardware-agnostic design patterns for scaling with problem size\n\nImplementation details:\n- Initialize with parent sequence and four other families (cosine, gaussian, etc.)\n- Use Phase 1: normalize to length 512 and apply mirror descent with eta_scale=0.5, max_iters=4000\n- Use Phase 2: adapt to length 1000 using gradient-based search (\u2207* = -D(u)/dt | t=0)\n- Use Phase 3: attempt variable-scale optimization (binary search around ratio=0.7)\n\nPseudocode outline:\ndef propose_candidate():\n    start_time = time.time()\n    deadline = start_time + 1500\n    \n    candidates_init = generate_all_initializations(parent_sequence)\n    best_seq = None\n    best_score = float('inf')\n    \n    # Phase 1: Fixed-length optimization\n    apply_mirror_descent_to_all(candidates_init, deadline, eta_scale=0.5)\n    # Phase 2: Gradient-based optimization\n    apply_gradient_descent_to_best_seq(candidates_init, deadline, family_\u03c3_min=0.25)\n    # Phase 3: Variable-scale\n    do_binary_search_on_length(candidates_init, deadline, ratio=0.7)\n    \n    return best_seq.tolist()\n\nThe keys to efficiency are:\n1. Using logarithmic transformations where appropriate\n2. Maintaining independent sets of function values\n3. Prioritizing candidate sequences by score before incurring refinement costs\n4. Setting per-iteration time budgets that scale with problem size\n5. Varied initialization strategies to pre-distribute search effort\n\nWith evaluation parameters of precision and avoidance of undefined (NaN/infinite) values, and sticking to integer arithmetic throughout implementation for compatibility.",
